{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>\"Behold now, I know…\" / Say now, you are my sister.</b> I do not know why he feared them now more than before. Therefore, if we say that the Egyptians were dark and ugly, as Rashi's words say, behold — he also said the same thing to Avimelech king of the Philistines, and so did Yitzhak, who dwelt in that land by the command of HaShem. Perhaps the Canaanites in that generation were steeped in idolatry and restrained from sexual immorality more than the people of Egypt and the Philistines. But this is not correct, and it is possible that they had no fear except when entering a city, the seat of kings, for it was their way to bring the very beautiful woman to the king and to kill her husband on a pretext they would fabricate against him. And the correct view in my eyes is that this was their practice from the time they left Haran: in every place he would say, \"she is my sister,\" for so he said (Genesis 20:13), \"and it came to pass, when Elohim caused me to wander from my father's house.\" But Scripture mentions this in the places where a new matter arises concerning it. And behold, he urged her now just as he had instructed her at the outset. And Yitzhak did not fear in his own land and city; only when he came to the land of the Philistines did he follow his father's way. And he said, \"that it may go well with me for your sake and that my soul may live because of you\" — all the days of our sojourning in this land until the famine passes, for Avraham came to sojourn in the land because of the famine, and when the famine passes he will return to the land concerning which he was commanded, which HaShem will give to him and to his seed, and he thought that their souls would live through the famine, and relief and deliverance would come to them from Elohim to return, or that it might be possible for them to flee to the land of Canaan when they despaired of it. And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 12:11): \"Behold now I know\" — a midrash aggadah: until now he did not recognize her beauty because of her modesty, and now through the journey. Another interpretation: through travel a person becomes disheveled, yet she maintained her beauty. And according to the plain sense: \"Behold now\" — the time has arrived when one must worry about your beauty, and \"I have known this for many days, for you are a woman beautiful of appearance,\" and now we are coming among dark people, brothers of the Cushites, who are not accustomed to a beautiful woman; and it is similar to \"Behold now, my lord, turn aside now\" — all this is the language of the Rav. And the midrash is a received tradition concerning the humility within them, and it is supported by Scripture, but there is no need for any of these things, for the word \"na\" (now) does not indicate a matter that is newly arising at that time alone; rather, for any ongoing and standing matter they say thus, for it alludes to the matter, to say that it is so now: \"Behold now I know that you are a woman beautiful of appearance\" — from then until now. And so too, \"Behold now, HaShem has restrained me from bearing\" (Genesis 16:2) — from my youth until this day. And so too, \"Behold now, I have two daughters\" (Genesis 19:8), for they were not born now — all of them are like this. And it appears from the plain sense of Scripture that Sarah did not accept upon herself to say thus; rather, the Egyptians were very wicked and sinful, and when they saw her and praised her to Pharaoh, she was taken to his house, and they did not ask them at all whether she was his wife or whether she was his sister, and she remained silent and did not say that she was his wife, and Avraham of his own accord said that she was his sister, and therefore they dealt well with him on her account. And this is what Scripture says: \"What is this you have done to me? Why did you not tell me that she was your wife?\" (Genesis 12:18) — he blamed him, for upon seeing that they were taking her, he should have told Pharaoh that she was his wife; and he went back and blamed him for saying afterward to the officers and to Pharaoh's household that she was his sister. And he did not blame the woman at all, for it is not fitting for her to contradict her husband, and the fitting thing for her is to remain silent."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:28:51.209974449Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}